Knox", "firstName": "Sarah M", "initials": "SM", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Program in Craniofacial Biology, Department of Cell and Tissue Biology, School of Dentistry, University of California, San Francisco, CA, U.S.A. Electronic address: CONTACT_EMAIL."}]}}, {"fullName": "Gokcumen O", "lastName": "Gokcumen", "firstName": "Omer", "initials": "O", "authorId": {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0003-4371-679X"}, "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Department of Biological Sciences, University at Buffalo, The State University of New York, Buffalo, NY, U.S.A. Electronic address: CONTACT_EMAIL."}]}}]}, "authorIdList": {"authorId": [{"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0001-5286-604X"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-8794-3217"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0003-2986-4630"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0003-4371-679X"}]}, "dataLinksTagsList": {"dataLinkstag": ["altmetrics", "supporting_data", "protocols", "related_data"]}, "journalInfo": {"issue": "7", "volume": "33", "journalIssueId": 3054496, "dateOfPublication": "2020 Nov", "monthOfPublication": 11, "yearOfPublication": 2020, "journal": {"medlineAbbreviation": "Cell Rep", "title": "Cell reports", "essn": "2211-1247", "issn": "2211-1247", "isoabbreviation": "Cell Rep", "nlmid": "101573691"}, "printPublicationDate": "2020-11-01"}, "pubYear": "2020", "pageInfo": "108402", "abstractText": "Salivary proteins are essential for maintaining health in the oral cavity and proximal digestive tract, and they serve as potential diagnostic markers for monitoring human health and disease. However, their precise organ origins remain unclear. Through transcriptomic analysis of major adult and fetal salivary glands and integration with the saliva proteome, the blood plasma proteome, and transcriptomes of 28+ organs, we link human saliva proteins to their source, identify salivary-gland-specific genes, and uncover fetal- and adult-specific gene repertoires. Our results also provide insights into the degree of gene retention during gland maturation and suggest that functional diversity among adult gland types is driven by specific dosage combinations of hundreds of transcriptional regulators rather than by a few gland-specific factors. Finally, we demonstrate the heterogeneity of the human acinar cell lineage. Our results pave the way for future investigations into glandular biology and pathology, as well as saliva's use as a diagnostic fluid.", "affiliation": "Department of Biological Sciences, University at Buffalo, The State University of New York, Buffalo, NY, U.S.A; Section of Genetic Medicine, Department of Medicine, University of Chicago, Chicago, IL, U.S.A; Faculty of Biosciences, Norwegian University of Life Sciences, \u00c5s, Viken, Norway.", "publicationStatus": "ppublish", "language": "eng", "pubModel": "Print", "pubTypeList": {"pubType": ["research-article", "Research Support, U.S. Gov't, Non-P.H.S.", "Journal Article", "Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]}, "grantsList": {"grant": [{"agency": "NIDCR NIH HHS", "acronym": "DE", "grantId": "R35 DE028255", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "Tobacco-Related Disease Research Program", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NIDCR NIH HHS", "acronym": "DE", "grantId": "R01 DE019807", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "National Institutes of Health", "orderIn": 0}]}, "meshHeadingList": {"meshHeading": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Mouth", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "ME", "qualifierName": "metabolism"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Salivary Glands", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "abbreviation": "ME", "qualifierName": "metabolism"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "PH", "qualifierName": "physiology"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Saliva", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "abbreviation": "ME", "qualifierName": "meta